# =============================================================================
# docker/.env — DEPLOYMENT credentials for Docker Compose
# =============================================================================
# Copy to docker/.env, or let `bash scripts/setup/generate-secrets.sh` write it
# for you (recommended — it generates real random values and chmod 600s them).
#
# WHY THIS FILE LIVES HERE AND NOT AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT
# -------------------------------------------------------
# Compose reads `.env` from the PROJECT DIRECTORY, which is the directory of
# the first `-f` file — that is `docker/`, not the repository root. A root
# `.env` is NEVER consulted for ${VAR} substitution. This caused a deployment
# that failed with "POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD is required" while the
# operator was staring at a root .env that plainly contained it.
#
# Two files, two jobs, do not merge them:
#   /.env         application settings, read by pydantic INSIDE the container
#   /docker/.env  deployment credentials, used ONLY for ${VAR} interpolation
#                 and never injected into a container
#
# Everything here is consumed at `docker compose` parse time on the host.
# =============================================================================

# --- Required by the production stacks (compose refuses to render without) ---
# docker-compose.prod.yml and docker-compose.prod.gpu.yml

# The exact origin browsers will use. Must match the TLS certificate and the
# app's AUTH_ALLOWED_ORIGINS, or login is rejected as cross-origin.
PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://face-detector.internal

# PostgreSQL superuser — used for the initial cluster only. The application
# never connects with it (see db/roles.sql for the least-privilege roles).
POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=

# Least-privilege database roles, one per job.
FR_APP_PASSWORD=
FR_MIGRATOR_PASSWORD=
FR_READONLY_PASSWORD=
FR_BACKUP_PASSWORD=

# Redis ACL user passwords. NOT --requirepass: the ACL file stores SHA-256
# hashes, so the plaintext never appears in `docker inspect` or the host
# process list. See docker/redis/users.acl.example.
REDIS_PASSWORD=
REDIS_MONITOR_PASSWORD=

# Grafana admin. Production has no default and will not start without it.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- Optional: every one of these has a working default in the compose file --

# Grafana admin username (default: admin)
# GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin

# Public URL Grafana builds links with (default: http://localhost:3000)
# GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=https://face-detector.internal:3000

# Alembic revision the API verifies at startup. Bump it in the SAME change as
# any new migration. deploy.sh derives this automatically from alembic/versions
# and writes it to docker/.env - set it by hand only to pin an older head
# deliberately. (default: the current head)
# MIGRATIONS_EXPECTED_HEAD=c5e7f9a1b3d4

# Backup cadence and retention for the prod `backup` service.
# (defaults: 86400 = daily, 14 days)
# BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=86400
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14

# Reuse an existing host directory of Ollama models instead of the named
# volume — avoids re-pulling several GB, and is how you seed an air-gapped
# host. (default: the ollama_models named volume)
# OLLAMA_MODELS_PATH=/srv/ollama-models
# OLLAMA_MODELS_PATH=C:/Users/you/.ollama

# GPU assignment, written by deploy.sh (comma-separated nvidia-smi indexes;
# resolved to stable GPU UUIDs in docker/gpu-allocation.generated.yml).
# First id -> face_recognition, second -> ollama. Leave unset for automatic
# allocation; set explicitly to override.
# GPU_IDS=0,1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development-only hosted LLM for the SQL agent (NVIDIA NIM)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lets a developer test SQL generation against build.nvidia.com's free
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint instead of the local Ollama models. Get a free
# key at https://build.nvidia.com (starts with nvapi-). Both lines must be
# set; Ollama remains the fallback.
#
# DEVELOPMENT ONLY. The prompts carry the database schema and user questions,
# so in production this would send them off a system documented as fully
# offline. A production boot REFUSES it (config guard, exit 78, no
# acknowledgement flag), the model registry registers no hosted model in
# production regardless of these values, and the switch cannot be set through
# the admin settings API. The production compose file does not even pass
# these variables through.
# LLM_DEV_PROVIDER=nim
# NVIDIA_NIM_API_KEY=nvapi-...
# NVIDIA_NIM_MODEL=meta/llama-3.2-11b-vision-instruct
# NVIDIA_NIM_SQL_MODEL=meta/llama-3.2-11b-vision-instruct
#
# Model availability on a free build.nvidia.com account CHANGES. As of
# 2026-08-30 only meta/llama-3.2-11b-vision-instruct is invocable here:
# openai/gpt-oss-120b authenticates and then hangs (150s+, no response),
# and llama-3.3-70b / qwen2.5-coder-32b / llama-3.1-70b / mixtral-8x22b /
# phi-3.5-mini are refused outright. If SQL-agent turns suddenly take
# minutes and the logs show '[STEP_2] planner failed: ... timed out',
# re-probe the endpoint and repoint these two lines — that symptom is a
# dead MODEL, not a dead provider.

# =============================================================================
# NOT here: ENVIRONMENT, WORKERS, DEBUG, LOG_LEVEL, DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL,
# JWT_SECRET_KEY, and the other ~90 application settings. Those are set
# explicitly per-stack inside the compose files so that each stack's security
# posture is auditable in one place, and putting them here would have no
# effect. See /.env.example for the application-level settings.
# =============================================================================
